        chwc     MEANS `s 183 the Crimes Against Humanity and War Crimes Act`
        customs  MEANS `s 183 the Customs Act`
        excise   MEANS `s 183 the Excise Act, 2001`
        eipa     MEANS `s 183 the Export and Import Permits Act`
        irpa     MEANS `s 183 the Immigration and Refugee Protection Act`
        fisia    MEANS `s 183 the Foreign Interference and Security of Information Act`
        tma      MEANS `s 183 the Trademarks Act`
        other    MEANS `s 183 an Act that is none of those`
        whole    MEANS `s 183 the whole section`
        part     MEANS `s 183 the subsection or paragraph named`
        act      MEANS `s 183 every offence under the Act`

GIVEN row IS A `s 183 row of the list of offences`
      charged IS A `s 183 provision of an Act`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 183 the row names the provision charged` MEANS
    IF row's `statute` EQUALS charged's `statute`
    THEN CONSIDER row's `reach`
         WHEN `s 183 every offence under the Act` THEN TRUE
         WHEN `s 183 the whole section` THEN row's `section` EQUALS charged's `section`
         WHEN `s 183 the subsection or paragraph named` THEN (row's `section` EQUALS charged's `section`) AND (row's `subsection` EQUALS charged's `subsection`)
    ELSE FALSE

GIVEN charged IS A `s 183 provision of an Act`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 183 the provision is in the list of offences` MEANS
    any (GIVEN row YIELD `s 183 the row names the provision charged` row charged) `s 183 the list of offences`

-- The lead-in of the definition: "an offence contrary to, any conspiracy or attempt to commit or being an accessory after the fact in relation to an offence contrary to, or any counselling in relation to
-- an offence contrary to" any of the listed provisions: the five forms of the charge are the keel's `s 2 form of the charge` (the same five appear in s 2 "criminal organization offence" and "terrorism offence").
-- FORK (F1): "means ... and includes" is read as closed (the keel's reading A): an offence that is neither in the list nor within the closing words is JUST FALSE.
-- FORK (F2): the closing words are two judgments ("reasonable grounds to believe"); each is a plain fact the person states. The second is narrower than the keel's `s 2 terrorism offence`: it is only paragraph (b) or (c) of that
-- definition, and `s 2 terrorism offence` does not say which paragraph fired, so it is its own input (a seam the plan lacks: see NOTES.md, Seams).
-- src:3527 | offence means an offence contrary to, any conspiracy or attempt to commit or being an accessory after the fact in relation to an offence contrary to, or any counselling in relation to an offence contrary to
-- src:3738 | and includes any other offence that there are reasonable grounds to believe is a criminal organization offence or any other offence that there are reasonable grounds to believe is an offence described in paragraph (b) or (c) of the definition terrorism offence in section 2; (infraction)
@ref Criminal Code s 183 "offence" (lead-in and closing words) (src:3527-3738)
GIVEN form IS A `s 2 form of the charge`
      `the offence charged, or the offence the charge concerns` IS A `s 183 provision of an Act`
      `there are reasonable grounds to believe that it is a criminal organization offence` IS A BOOLEAN
      `there are reasonable grounds to believe that it is an offence described in paragraph (b) or (c) of the definition terrorism offence in section 2` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 183 offence` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `there are reasonable grounds to believe that it is a criminal organization offence`
        THEN JUST TRUE
        IF `there are reasonable grounds to believe that it is an offence described in paragraph (b) or (c) of the definition terrorism offence in section 2`
        THEN JUST TRUE
        IF (form EQUALS `a form of participation that is none of those`)
        THEN JUST FALSE   -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "offence means an offence contrary to, any conspiracy or attempt to commit or being an accessory after the fact in relation to an offence contrary to, or any counselling in relation to an offence contrary to"
        IF `s 183 the provision is in the list of offences` `the offence charged, or the offence the charge concerns`
        THEN JUST TRUE
        OTHERWISE JUST FALSE   -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "offence means an offence contrary to"
